+# tidyjson
+
+A small command-line tool that reformats a JSON file in place.
+
+> **Status:** `cli.js` currently contains only the usage header, with no implementation. This README describes the interface that header specifies. Running the command right now does nothing.
+
+## What it does
+
+`tidyjson` reads a JSON file, re-serializes it with consistent indentation (and, if you ask, with keys sorted), and writes the result back to the same file.
+
+If the file is not valid JSON, `tidyjson` leaves it **untouched** and exits with status `2`.
+
+## Installation
+
+Requires [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/).
+
+```sh
+# from a clone of this repository
+npm install -g .
+
+# or run without installing
+node cli.js <file> [options]
+```
+
+## Usage
+
+```sh
+tidyjson <file> [--indent N] [--sort-keys]
+```
+
+| Argument / option | Description |
+| --- | --- |
+| `<file>` | Path to the JSON file to rewrite. The file is modified in place. |
+| `--indent N` | Indent with `N` spaces. |
+| `--sort-keys` | Sort object keys alphabetically. |
+
+### Examples
+
+```sh
+# Reformat a file
+tidyjson config.json
+
+# Use 4-space indentation
+tidyjson config.json --indent 4
+
+# Sort keys and use 2-space indentation
+tidyjson package-lock.json --indent 2 --sort-keys
+```
+
+Given `data.json`:
+
+```json
+{"name":"demo","version":1,"tags":["a","b"]}
+```
+
+`tidyjson data.json --indent 2 --sort-keys` rewrites it as:
+
+```json
+{
+  "name": "demo",
+  "tags": [
+    "a",
+    "b"
+  ],
+  "version": 1
+}
+```
+
+## Exit codes
+
+| Code | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `0` | The file was reformatted successfully. |
+| `2` | The file contains invalid JSON. It was not modified. |
+
+Because invalid input never changes the file, you can safely run `tidyjson` in scripts or pre-commit hooks and check the exit code:
+
+```sh
+tidyjson settings.json || echo "settings.json is not valid JSON"
+```
+
+## Development
+
+Run the tests with Node's built-in test runner:
+
+```sh
+npm test
+```
